pper sulfide precipitation. Exact experimental catalyst loading is not supplied."}, "mechanism": "Copper-mediated addition of HCN across acetylene produces the C3 unsaturated nitrile.", "reasoning": "Supported in the supplied account of Patel et al. (2015). Cu(I) has a reaction-specific role rather than being an arbitrary mineral additive. Acetylene availability, local copper concentration, cyanide speciation, and staged delivery remain geochemical constraints. Acetylene is supplied here; its proposed carbide source is not assumed to have been demonstrated."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Ammonia aza-Michael addition to acrylonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Ammonia-rich solution; proposed pH 8-10. Limit prolonged exposure that could favor oligomerization or repeated alkylation."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia adds conjugatively to the activated alkene, followed by proton transfer.", "reasoning": "This branch is described by Patel et al. (2015). It introduces the nitrogen ultimately retained in the pyrrolidine ring. Cyanamide is not included because guanidinylation would divert this branch toward arginine-related precursors rather than improve proline formation."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Photoreductive conversion of beta-aminopropionitrile to its aldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_005", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_003", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "UV irradiation; 254 nm is the laboratory analogue. H2S/HS- supplies reducing equivalents. Proposed near-neutral to mildly alkaline conditions; no molecular oxygen."}, "mechanism": "Photochemical nitrile reduction gives an imine that hydrolyzes to the aldehyde, releasing ammonia.", "reasoning": "Supported in outline by Patel et al. (2015); exact proline-branch conditions require supplementary-information verification. Copper acceleration is not assumed because sulfide-driven chemistry can operate without it. Sulfur is represented by an idealized S8 endpoint; actual sulfur speciation and photon efficiency require measurement. Sulfide availability is constrained by Ranjan et al. (2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Cyanohydrin homologation of beta-aminopropionaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "HCN/CN- availability with controlled addition; proposed pH 7-9. Minimize aldehyde residence time before capture."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide attacks the aldehyde and protonation produces a racemic cyanohydrin.", "reasoning": "This C1 homologation is part of the sequence summarized from Patel et al. (2015). It gives the four-carbon precursor needed for a five-membered nitrogen heterocycle. An achiral environment provides no basis for assigning one cyanohydrin configuration preferentially."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Cyanohydrin sulfurization and cyclic hydroxythioamide formation", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Sulfide-bearing solution; staged equilibration. Exact pH and cyclic-to-open-chain product ratio are not specified in the supplied evidence."}, "mechanism": "Sulfide addition to the nitrile generates an open-chain hydroxythioamide; intramolecular aminolysis forms the five-membered thiolactam and releases ammonia.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015), as summarized, observed cyclic and open-chain alpha-hydroxythioamides. This is a net reaction node combining sulfur addition and ring closure, not a claim that the cyclic product forms exclusively or in one elementary step. The unproven productive fate of the open-chain coproduct is not separately counted as a pathway."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Photodeoxygenation of the cyclic hydroxythioamide", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_004", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "UV irradiation with continuing H2S/HS- supply; 254 nm laboratory analogue. Irradiation dose under realistic solar spectra is unresolved."}, "mechanism": "Photoreductive replacement of the alpha-hydroxyl group by hydrogen gives gamma-butyrothiolactam.", "reasoning": "The supplied Patel et al. (2015) account identifies the cyclic thiolactam as the only deoxygenated thioamide detected in this mixture. Sulfide is a reducing reagent, not an unspecified mineral catalyst. Geochemical sulfide availability and realistic irradiation rates remain bottlenecks (Ranjan et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Thiolactam photoreduction through an inferred cyclic imine", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_005", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "UV/H2S photoreductive medium; rapid subsequent HCN capture. H2S occurs on both sides because reductant oxidation and release of substrate sulfur as H2S can cancel in the formal net balance."}, "mechanism": "Reduction and sulfur elimination are represented as passing through 1-pyrroline before cyanide capture.", "reasoning": "The thiolactam-to-aminonitrile conversion is supported by Patel et al. (2015), but the supplied review explicitly identifies free 1-pyrroline as inferred rather than isolated. Separating this node from HCN capture is mechanistic bookkeeping. Neither an isolated imine yield nor an exclusive S8 coproduct distribution is established."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Cyclic Strecker capture of 1-pyrroline", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "HCN addition during or immediately after imine generation; competing imine hydrolysis must be limited."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide attacks the cyclic imine or iminium carbon, creating the proline aminonitrile stereocenter without enantioselection.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) reported the proline aminonitrile; the supplied account gives 32% over two late precursor-forming steps, not a yield for isolated 1-pyrroline or proline. Ritson (2021) notes unusual resistance of this cyclic aminonitrile to retro-Strecker conversion under conditions that destabilize acyclic analogues. Its formation is racemic."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Acid-catalyzed proline aminonitrile hydration", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_015"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-370 K; proposed hydrolysis window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Acidic geothermal pool, proposed pH 1-3; transition to hydrolysis only after aminonitrile formation. Required duration and conversion are unknown."}, "mechanism": "Nitrile protonation, water addition, and tautomerization produce the carboxamide.", "reasoning": "Chemically expected but unquantified for this network. The supplied Patel et al. (2015) account stops at the aminonitrile and does not establish a prolinamide yield. Ritson (2021) supports precursor retention, not a measured hydration rate. The proposed conditions are a testable hypothesis rather than a reproduced prebiotic protocol."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Prolinamide hydrolysis to racemic proline", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_017", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K; proposed hydrolysis window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Sustained acidic aqueous residence; acid-base forms of ammonia and proline are implicit. No stereochemical separation is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Water attacks the activated amide carbonyl, and ammonia departure gives the carboxylic acid.", "reasoning": "Standard amide-hydrolysis chemistry supplies a plausible final conversion, but the provided research contains no end-to-end hydrolysis yield from the Patel et al. (2015) proline precursor. This is an unquantified bottleneck. L- and D-proline are both outputs because racemic precursor hydrolysis does not establish homochirality."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Candidate conjugate hydrocyanation of acrylonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_019"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 8-10 supplies a nucleophilic cyanide fraction. Competing acrylonitrile polymerization and reversibility require measurement."}, "mechanism": "Conjugate cyanide addition followed by protonation generates the C4 dinitrile skeleton.", "reasoning": "Speculative as a prebiotic network edge: no supplied study demonstrates this reaction under the stated geological conditions. It is included because an activated alkene and cyanide provide a chemically direct alternative to the aldehyde/cyanohydrin homologation, not because Patel et al. (2015) established this branch."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Candidate selective monoreduction of succinonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_019", "mol_018"], "outputs": ["mol_020"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Native nickel or a nickel-rich metallic surface"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-400 K; proposed screening window", "pressure": "10-100 bar total pressure with an H2-rich fluid", "catalyst": "Metallic nickel surface", "aqueous": true, "other": "Sulfide-poor rock-water pocket; short residence and limited conversion would be needed to suppress reduction of both nitriles."}, "mechanism": "Surface hydrogenation converts one nitrile into a primary amine while retaining the other nitrile.", "reasoning": "Speculative and selectivity-limited. Nickel can activate H2, but the supplied literature does not demonstrate prebiotic succinonitrile monoreduction. Kaur et al. (2024) support nickel/H2 amino-acid-related chemistry, not this substrate or selectivity. Diamine formation, cyclization, overreduction, and nickel poisoning could prevent useful accumulation."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Candidate photoreductive hydrolysis of gamma-aminobutyronitrile", "inputs": ["mol_020", "mol_005", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_021", "mol_003", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "UV/H2S exposure after transfer from a sulfide-poor reduction setting; 254 nm laboratory analogue. Minimize aldehyde residence time."}, "mechanism": "Partial nitrile reduction followed by imine hydrolysis gives 4-aminobutanal.", "reasoning": "Speculative substrate extension of the nitrile photoreduction chemistry described by Patel et al. (2015). No supplied experiment establishes this gamma-amino substrate conversion. Sulfur addition and five-membered thiolactam formation may outcompete aldehyde production; rxn_015-rxn_016 explicitly represent that chemically different possibility."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "4-Aminobutanal cyclocondensation", "inputs": ["mol_021"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Near-neutral solution with immediate HCN trapping; dilution and imine hydrolysis compete with net accumulation."}, "mechanism": "The terminal amino group attacks the aldehyde, followed by dehydration to the five-membered cyclic imine.", "reasoning": "Chemically well-motivated intramolecular imine formation, but not an isolated discharge intermediate established by the supplied sources. Ring et al. (1972) support proline detection, not this mechanistic assignment. Its role here is conditional on independently producing 4-aminobutanal."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Candidate sulfurization of gamma-aminobutyronitrile", "inputs": ["mol_020", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_022"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Sulfide-bearing concentrated solution; proposed mildly alkaline conditions. No ultraviolet requirement is assumed for the addition step."}, "mechanism": "Hydrosulfide addition to the nitrile and proton transfer form the open-chain thioamide.", "reasoning": "Speculative for this substrate. Patel et al. (2015) provide related nitrile-to-thioamide chemistry, but do not establish this branch in the supplied account. It is chemically motivated by the same sulfur nucleophile and provides a testable alternative to attempting selective aldehyde production."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Candidate cyclization of gamma-aminobutanethioamide", "inputs": ["mol_022"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "320-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrating geothermal pool; proposed moderate pH allowing a nucleophilic terminal amine. Ammonia removal could favor ring closure."}, "mechanism": "Intramolecular attack on the thiocarbonyl followed by ammonia elimination produces a five-membered thiolactam.", "reasoning": "Speculative but supported by favorable ring geometry and the related hydroxythioamide cyclization reported by Patel et al. (2015). The unsubstituted open-chain thioamide is not demonstrated as a productive precursor in the supplied research. Rate, equilibrium position, and competing hydrolysis remain unknown."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Candidate partial hydrogenation and intramolecular transimination", "inputs": ["mol_020", "mol_018"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Native nickel or a nickel-rich metallic surface"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-400 K; proposed screening window", "pressure": "10-100 bar total pressure with controlled H2 activity", "catalyst": "Metallic nickel surface", "aqueous": true, "other": "Sulfide-poor pocket; imine must desorb and leave the reducing zone before further hydrogenation. HCN capture is assigned to a subsequent surface stage."}, "mechanism": "Partial nitrile hydrogenation gives an open-chain imine; intramolecular transimination releases ammonia and forms 1-pyrroline.", "reasoning": "Speculative: no supplied study demonstrates this route under prebiotic conditions. The mechanism avoids an aldehyde intermediate, making it distinct from rxn_013-rxn_014. Nickel/H2 plausibility alone is insufficient evidence (Kaur et al., 2024); overreduction to pyrrolidine or formation of a diamine may dominate."}, {"id": "rxn_018", "name": "Candidate sulfur-assisted conversion of proline aminonitrile to thioamide", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_023"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated sulfide-bearing solution; proposed pH 8-10. Assess aminonitrile recovery and competing reactions explicitly."}, "mechanism": "Sulfide adds to the exocyclic nitrile, giving the corresponding carbothioamide without changing the pyrrolidine skeleton.", "reasoning": "Speculative alternative to direct nitrile hydration. Patel et al. (2015) support related thioamide chemistry, not this proline substrate transformation. Ritson (2021) supports unusual aminonitrile persistence but does not establish sulfur-assisted hydrolysis. No rate advantage or yield improvement is asserted."}, {"id": "rxn_019", "name": "Candidate prolinethioamide hydrolysis", "inputs": ["mol_023", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_017", "mol_003", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K; proposed screening window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Acidic geothermal water after sulfurization; prolonged residence may be required. Net hydrolysis consumes two waters per thioamide."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolytic replacement of sulfur and subsequent amide cleavage yield the carboxylic acid, hydrogen sulfide, and ammonia.", "reasoning": "Speculative for prebiotic prolinethioamide: no supplied study demonstrates this conversion or its rate. The net chemistry is a chemically reasonable sulfur-assisted alternative, not an established extension of Patel et al. (2015). A prolinamide intermediate may occur transiently; the node represents the overall hydrolysis sequence. Both proline enantiomers remain present."}, {"id": "rxn_020", "name": "Candidate aldol assembly of a C5 hydroxy keto acid", "inputs": ["mol_024", "mol_025"], "outputs": ["mol_026"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide ions as a proposed general-base catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "General-base catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated pyruvate and glyoxylate; proposed pH 8-10. Competing self-condensation, hydration, and decomposition require measurement."}, "mechanism": "A pyruvate-derived enol or enolate attacks glyoxylate, producing 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate.", "reasoning": "Speculative stepwise assignment in this network. Kaur et al. (2024) support coupling reductive aldol keto-acid generation with amino-acid chemistry, but the supplied summary does not verify isolation of this intermediate under these conditions. This edge provides a chemically motivated C2+C3 supply hypothesis rather than assuming an unexplained C5 feedstock."}, {"id": "rxn_021", "name": "Candidate selective reductive deoxygenation to alpha-ketoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_026", "mol_018"], "outputs": ["mol_027", "mol_004"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Native nickel or a nickel-rich metallic surface"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-400 K; proposed screening window", "pressure": "10-100 bar total pressure with H2", "catalyst": "Metallic nickel surface", "aqueous": true, "other": "Sulfide-poor conditions. Desired hydroxyl removal must compete successfully with reduction of the keto group and carbon-carbon bond cleavage."}, "mechanism": "Net replacement of the C4 hydroxyl group by hydrogen generates alpha-ketoglutarate.", "reasoning": "Speculative substrate-specific edge. The reductive aldol framework discussed by Kaur et al. (2024) motivates testing this connection but does not, in the supplied summary, establish this isolated transformation. Selective deoxygenation while retaining an alpha-keto acid is a substantial unresolved requirement."}, {"id": "rxn_022", "name": "Metal-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_027", "mol_003", "mol_018"], "outputs": ["mol_028", "mol_004"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Metallic nickel"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-373 K proposed; the cited mild nickel study operated near 295 K", "pressure": "10-100 bar total pressure; proposed H2 partial pressure 1-5 bar", "catalyst": "Metallic nickel", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 8-10 and locally concentrated ammonium/ammonia. Maintain a reduced, sulfide-poor catalytic surface."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia condensation with the alpha-keto group followed by hydrogenation gives glutamate.", "reasoning": "Glutamate formation by abiotic reductive amination has experimental support from Kitadai et al. (2019) and Kaur et al. (2024). The stated warmer hydrothermal window is an extrapolation, not a reproduced yield claim. These studies support glutamate formation only; they do not establish the subsequent proline steps. Achiral catalysis gives racemic glutamate."}, {"id": "rxn_023", "name": "Unresolved glutamate side-chain carboxylate reduction", "inputs": ["mol_028", "mol_018"], "outputs": ["mol_029", "mol_004"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "Not established; 350-400 K is only a possible screening window", "pressure": "Not established; an H2-rich pressurized aqueous setting is hypothetical", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "No validated nonenzymatic activating agent or selective reducing system is supplied. This edge explicitly denotes an unresolved research gap, not an executable reaction."}, "mechanism": "Formal reduction of the side-chain carboxyl group to an aldehyde while retaining the alpha-carboxyl group.", "reasoning": "Speculative and currently unsupported as a prebiotic synthesis. Biological glutamate reduction requires activation and reducing cofactors (Fichman et al., 2015). The supplied review identifies no mineral or metal system accomplishing this selective conversion. Favorable overall amino-acid formation energetics cannot remove this kinetic and activation barrier (Amend and Shock, 1998). Consequently P9 and P10 are not established complete chemical routes."}, {"id": "rxn_024", "name": "Spontaneous glutamate semialdehyde cyclization", "inputs": ["mol_029"], "outputs": ["mol_030", "mol_004"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "350-373 K proposed for a cool hydrothermal compartment", "pressure": "10-100 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Near-neutral to mildly alkaline aqueous conditions; short residence before further reaction. The cited spontaneous cyclization is not specific to this pressure-temperature window."}, "mechanism": "Intramolecular amino-aldehyde condensation closes a five-membered ring and eliminates water.", "reasoning": "Spontaneous, strongly favored GSA/P5C interconversion is supported by Smith and Greenberg (1956). No enzyme is needed for this ring closure. This established local transformation does not solve GSA supply, and its inclusion must not be interpreted as evidence for the preceding carboxylate reduction."}, {"id": "rxn_025", "name": "Candidate nickel-hydrogen reduction of P5C", "inputs": ["mol_030", "mol_018"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_017"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Metallic nickel"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-373 K; proposed screening window", "pressure": "10-100 bar total pressure with H2", "catalyst": "Metallic nickel", "aqueous": true, "other": "Sulfide-poor conditions; monitor substrate adsorption, decomposition, and recovery. No biological reductase or modern hydride reagent is used."}, "mechanism": "Hydrogenation of the cyclic imine bond yields the saturated pyrrolidine ring.", "reasoning": "Speculative prebiotic implementation. P5C reduction is chemically plausible, but the supplied review found no demonstrated H2/Ni prebiotic conversion to proline. Kaur et al. (2024) justify investigating nickel/H2 chemistry, not assigning a P5C yield. Racemic GSA/P5C supply gives both proline enantiomers; reduction does not create an L-selective route."}, {"id": "rxn_026", "name": "Candidate sulfide photoreduction of P5C", "inputs": ["mol_030", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_017", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Requires delivery of P5C-containing fluid into a UV-accessible sulfide-bearing pool. Use a 254 nm laboratory analogue only as a test system; realistic solar performance is unknown."}, "mechanism": "Proposed photogenerated reducing equivalents hydrogenate the cyclic imine while sulfide is oxidized.", "reasoning": "Speculative: Patel et al. (2015) demonstrate related sulfide photoreduction chemistry, but the supplied review found no prebiotic P5C-to-proline demonstration. This is mechanistically distinct from nickel/H2 hydrogenation. P5C photodegradation, sulfide addition, and geochemical sulfide limitations could defeat the proposed reduction (Ranjan et al., 2018). No enantioselection is predicted."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Reported cyanosulfidic skeleton construction with conventional hydrolysis", "description": "Best-supported intermediate-resolved skeleton route. The cyclic imine is inferred, and aminonitrile-to-proline hydrolysis remains unquantified. Produces L-proline within a racemate, not selectively.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_012", "mol_014", "mol_015"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Cyanosulfidic skeleton construction with candidate sulfur-assisted hydrolysis", "description": "Shares P1's skeleton chemistry but replaces conventional nitrile hydration with an unvalidated prolinethioamide route. A distinct mechanistic variant, not an independently demonstrated synthesis.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_018", "rxn_019"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_011", "mol_012", "mol_014", "mol_023"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Candidate succinonitrile-to-aminoaldehyde route with conventional hydrolysis", "description": "Builds the C4 skeleton by conjugate hydrocyanation, selectively reduces one nitrile, and photore