m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: L-Proline\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Construct an evidence-graded network containing ten distinct reaction sequences, not ten independently demonstrated prebiotic syntheses. P1 follows the reported cyanosulfidic construction of the proline aminonitrile, followed by chemically expected but unquantified hydrolysis. P2-P8 are explicitly speculative mechanistic alternatives involving nitrile reduction, sulfurization, cyclization, or sulfur-assisted hydrolysis. P9-P10 explore a glutamate/P5C route and contain an explicitly unresolved carboxylate-reduction step; they are research hypotheses, not established syntheses. Small C2/C3 keto acids are supplied feedstocks for those two paths, not claimed products of CO2 fixation. All achiral routes produce L-proline together with D-proline; separate stereoisomer nodes prevent a racemate from being mislabeled as enantiopure L-proline. This network therefore reaches L-proline as a racemate component, not isolated or selectively synthesized L-proline. No demonstrated prebiotic resolution step is supplied: proline phase amplification requires an initial bias, and the strongest reported enrichment used non-prebiotic chloroform (Klussmann et al., 2006). Conditions are proposed geological operating windows unless explicit