action as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.66\n- Feedback: The network is structurally strong: network validity is approximately 0.97, with sufficient nodes, consistent molecule references and a connected graph. Accepting the deterministic check as ground truth, all 26 reactions conserve atoms and charge with appropriate coefficients; there are no conservation violations. Multiples of eight arising from the S8 reference endpoint are not intrinsically implausible. Explicit coefficients should nevertheless be recorded, with stereochemical product ratios constrained independently of elemental balancing. Literature alignment is good, approximately 0.83: the network appropriately distinguishes reported precursor chemistry, inferred intermediates, speculative alternatives, unquantified hydrolysis and unresolved carboxylate reduction. It also correctly avoids converting detection-only studies into invented mechanisms. However, reaction-specific citations and verified experimental procedures are largely absent. The strongest chemical support concerns the P1 aminonitrile-forming sequence, not a demonstrated end-to-end geological synthesis of free proline. Conventional hydrolysis makes L-proline production as a racemate component credible, but its performance in the actual reaction mixture remains unmeasured. P2-P8 require additional selectivity and substrate-specific evidence; P9-P10 are blocked by rxn_023. Environmental assessment is limited because the submitted records provide broad environment labels rather than numerical conditions, catalyst availability, reagent staging or physically justified transfers. Copper speciation in sulfidic media, hydrogenation catalyst activity, hydrolysis conditions and intermediate survival require explicit treatment. These scores assess the complete network through its supported route and enabling bottlenecks, not by averaging duplicated pathway variants or rewarding any feedstock, framework, hub count or environmental order. This is a useful evidence-graded research network, but overall confidence is below the 0.90 network approval threshold.\n- Issues: ['This is the strongest pathway. Its aminonitrile construction follows the supplied account of Patel et al. (2015), including the reported 32% yield over the final two precursor-forming steps. The discrete 1-pyrroline intermediate remains inferred. Aminonitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis are conventional chemistry, but their rates, yields and compatibility with the preceding mixture are not established here. Surface labels alone do not specify the necessary operating conditions. L-proline is plausibly obtained as a racemate component; the absence of resolution is not treated as failure of that explicitly stated objective.', 'P2 retains the evidence-supported aminonitrile construction but adds an unvalidated sulfur-assisted finishing sequence. Nitrile sulfurization and thioamide hydrolysis are chemically recognizable transformations, and both equations conserve mass. However, the network does not establish that this substrate sulfurizes efficiently or that its thioamide hydrolyzes faster or under more suitable conditions than the original nitrile. Acknowledging these uncertainties is appropriate but does not establish productive flux to proline.', 'Conjugate cyanide addition to acrylonitrile is plausible, and 4-aminobutanal cyclization followed by Strecker capture is coherent. The principal enabling uncertainty is rxn_012: a catalyst and operating window that provide useful gamma-aminobutyronitrile flux rather than extensive further reduction are not specified. Reaction rxn_013 extrapolates established photoreductive chemistry to a different substrate. Transfers of succinonitrile into the hydrothermal setting and amino nitrile back to the surface lack transport and survival justification.', 'The carbon-skeleton sequence is coherent, but it combines the unestablished selective hydrogenation and substrate-specific photoreduction of P3 with the unvalidated sulfur-assisted finishing sequence of P2. None of these reactions violates the supplied conservation check. Nevertheless, their serial combination does not establish a working synthesis, and the proposed environment labels do not establish catalyst availability, transport or compatible sulfurization and hydrolysis conditions.', 'This pathway could connect an amino nitrile to the literature-supported thiolactam branch, but its entrance reactions remain conditional. Sulfurization is plausible, whereas rxn_016 requires demonstrated intramolecular thioamide substitution with ammonia elimination; favorable five-membered ring formation alone does not establish that transformation. Selective succinonitrile reduction remains an upstream bottleneck. The conventional hydrolysis endpoint is credible but unquantified.', 'P6 is a coherent research hypothesis but depends on several unverified enabling transformations: selective dinitrile reduction, amino-thioamide cyclization and sulfur-assisted aminonitrile hydrolysis. Reaching gamma-butyrothiolactam would connect to an experimentally supported precursor-forming segment, but that support does not validate the proposed upstream or downstream reactions. Sulfide participation in multiple steps does not by itself establish compatible operating conditions.', 'Partial nitrile hydrogenation followed by intramolecular transimination is mechanistically plausible and atom-balanced. The difficulty is accumulating pyrroline under conditions capable of reducing nitriles: the cyclic imine may undergo further hydrogenation to pyrrolidine before HCN capture. This selectivity problem compounds the preceding selective monoreduction requirement. No catalyst, hydrogen activity, quenching procedure or pyrroline transport lifetime is supplied.', 'P8 inherits the unresolved hydrogenation selectivity and pyrroline-survival problems of P7, then adds an unvalidated sulfur-assisted finishing sequence. The molecular connections and conservation bookkeeping are valid, but no evidence establishes that sufficient flux can pass through these successive selectivity gates. The speculative designation is accurate and improves literature honesty, not chemical validation.', 'The supplied keto-acid feedstocks are acceptable, and the C5 skeleton is coherent. Keto-acid reductive amination has experimental support, while glutamate semialdehyde cyclization is well established. However, rxn_023 is an unresolved enabling gap: selective aqueous glutamate side-chain carboxylate reduction cannot be assumed from H2 availability. Nickel-mediated P5C reduction is chemically plausible but not demonstrated under prebiotic conditions in the supplied review. Downstream plausibility cannot overcome the missing semialdehyde-forming step.', \"P10 remains blocked by rxn_023 and additionally requires substrate-specific validation of sulfide photoreduction and survival during P5C transfer to the surface. Reaction rxn_026 is chemically conceivable and balanceable, but the supplied literature does not demonstrate it. The checker's 7:1 L:D coefficient solution is only an elemental-balancing artifact; it provides no evidence for enantioenrichment and does not match the network's stated racemic outcome.\"]\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"L-Proline\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"The supplied evidence supports one intermediate-resolved candidate synthesis, not ten independently supported pathways. This network therefore contains one traced pathway rather than inventing nine additional routes or counting changes of catalyst, temperature, or workup as independent syntheses. It does not meet the requested ten-pathway count. The productive sequence is acetylene hydrocyanation, ammonia addition, sulfide-driven photoreductive homologation, cyclic thioamide formation, photochemical conversion to the proline aminonitrile, and hydrolysis through prolinamide. Experimental support is strongest through the aminonitrile; its hydrolysis in the actual reaction mixture remains unquantified. L-proline is represented explicitly as one component of racemic proline, alongside D-proline: this is not an enantioselective synthesis or an isolation of pure L-proline. An aqueous, geochemically established resolution step is unavailable from the supplied evidence, so none is invented. Numerical conditions below are proposed screening windows, not verified reproductions of the Patel supplementary procedures. All operations occupy adjacent surface environments; no transport of unstable intermediates through hydrothermal systems is assumed. Parent neutral formulas are used for component bookkeeping, while pH-dependent protonation is described in conditions. S8 is a formal elemental-sulfur endpoint, not a claim that sulfur speciation was experimentally established. The graph follows productive channels and necessary coproducts, not every component of the experimental mixtures. Because the prescribed role vocabulary lacks 'byproduct', non-target terminal coproducts are labeled 'intermediate'.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethyne\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetylene\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H2/c1-2/h1-2H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"sulfane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen sulfide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H3N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"prop-2-enenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acrylonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H3N/c1-2-3-4/h2H,1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-aminopropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"beta-aminopropionitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-aminopropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"beta-aminopropionaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"4-amino-2-hydroxybutanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"3-aminopropanal cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H7NOS\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-hydroxypyrrolidine-2-thione\",\n      \"common_name\": \"cyclic alpha-hydroxythioamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H7NS\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"pyrrolidine-2-thione\",\n      \"common_name\": \"gamma-butyrothiolactam\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H7N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3,4-dihydro-2H-pyrrole\",\n      \"common_name\": \"1-pyrroline\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H8N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"proline aminonitrile, unresolved R/S pool\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H10N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"pyrrolidine-2-carboxamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"prolinamide, unresolved R/S pool\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2S)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"L-proline\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c7-5(8)4-2-1-3-6-4/h4,6H,1-3H2,(H,7,8)/t4-/m0/s1\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2R)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"D-proline\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c7-5(8)4-2-1-3-6-4/h4,6H,1-3H2,(H,7,8)/t4-/m1/s1\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"S8\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"cyclooctasulfur\",\n      \"common_name\": \"elemental sulfur, formal redox endpoint\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu(I)-catalyzed hydrocyanation of acetylene\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_001\",\n        \"mol_002\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_006\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Dissolved Cu(I) chloride/cyanide complexes in a sulfide-poor reaction zone\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-360 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm, with replenished local acetylene-bearing gas\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I) complexes\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, chloride-bearing water; initial pH screening approximately 4-7. Maintain low sulfide activity during coupling. HCN and acetylene escape, CuCN precipitation, and acetylene side reactions must be measured. Transfer the acrylonitrile-containing liquid locally to the ammonia-addition zone; do not assume active Cu(I) survives subsequent sulfide exposure.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Copper-mediated addition of HCN across the acetylene triple bond produces acrylonitrile. The net component equation is HCN + C2H2 -> C3H3N.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The acetylene-to-acrylonitrile entry is described in the cyanosulfidic framework (Patel et al., 2015). Copper is justified specifically for this transformation, not added as a generic mineral catalyst. Its availability and speciation remain geochemical constraints: sulfide can remove dissolved copper, and concentrated cyanide can alter or precipitate copper complexes. The numerical window is a proposed test range because the supplied review did not verify the original experimental procedure.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia aza-Michael addition to acrylonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_006\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_007\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic water, approximately pH 9-10, with ammonia in excess over acrylonitrile. Ammonia-rich conditions are staged after copper-mediated coupling rather than assumed compatible with every upstream condition. Quantify secondary Michael addition, oligomerization, and ammonia loss. Do not introduce cyanamide, which would create a competing guanidinylation branch.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia attacks the beta carbon of acrylonitrile, followed by proton transfer to give 3-aminopropanenitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This addition supplies the nitrogen that ultimately becomes the pyrrolidine ring nitrogen and is part of the reported proline-precursor sequence (Patel et al., 2015). No mineral catalyst is required. Ammonia excess is chemically motivated by competition with further addition of the newly formed primary amine, but the required concentration in a natural pool is not established.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfide-driven photoreduction of the amino nitrile to an amino aldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_004\",\n        \"mol_005\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_003\",\n        \"mol_017\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, shallow irradiated water; proposed pH 7.5-9. H2S/HS\u2212 supplies reducing equivalents. Laboratory precedent uses 254 nm irradiation; a productive rate under an early-Earth solar spectrum must be tested separately. Do not require residual dissolved copper. Deliver HCN for immediate downstream aldehyde trapping; avoid isolation, storage, or hot transport of the amino aldehyde. Control optical attenuation by sulfur-bearing solids.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photogenerated reducing equivalents convert the nitrile into an imine-level intermediate, whose hydrolysis releases the aldehyde and ammonia. Formal sulfur-endpoint bookkeeping is 8 C3H6N2 + 8 H2S + 8 H2O -> 8 C3H7NO + 8 NH3 + S8.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Reductive homologation through aldehydes is supported within the cyanosulfidic framework (Patel et al., 2015). H2S-driven photochemistry need not assume an active Cu(I)/Cu(II) catalyst after sulfide addition, although substrate-specific performance without copper requires confirmation. Sulfidic-anion availability is a genuine environmental constraint rather than an unlimited reagent supply (Ranjan et al., 2018). Aldehyde accumulation, overreduction, and irradiation-dose dependence remain experimental questions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin trapping and one-carbon homologation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_009\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same local reaction volume as aldehyde formation, approximately pH 7-9. HCN/CN\u2212 must be available during aldehyde production. Monitor competing Strecker chemistry, aldehyde oligomerization, and cyanohydrin reversal. The hydroxyl-bearing stereocenter is unspecified and is subsequently erased by deoxygenation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to the aldehyde carbonyl and the alkoxide is protonated, forming 4-amino-2-hydroxybutanenitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyanohydrin formation extends the C3 precursor to the C4 skeleton needed for the ring and is part of the supplied reconstruction of Patel et al. (2015). Immediate capture is required because the amino aldehyde is not a credible transported or stockpiled intermediate. Cyanide concentration and ammonia competition must be evaluated in the actual mixture; a connected graph alone does not establish productive flux.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfide addition and formation of the cyclic alpha-hydroxythioamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic sulfide-bearing water; proposed pH screening 8-9.5. This node represents the productive cyclic-product channel, not quantitative conversion of the whole mixture. Open-chain hydroxythioamide formation competes. Excess ammonia from earlier stages may influence ring closure and must be measured rather than ignored.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sulfide addition to the nitrile enables thioimidate/thioamide chemistry and intramolecular capture by the terminal amino group. The net cyclic-product channel eliminates ammonia: C4H8N2O + H2S -> C4H7NOS + NH3. The exact order of the elementary sulfur-addition and ring-closure events is not asserted.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The supplied account reports cyclic and open-chain alpha-hydroxythioamides together (Patel et al., 2015). Consequently, this network does not invent an obligatory, independently demonstrated conversion of an isolated open-chain thioamide into the ring. Five-membered-ring formation explains the chemical opportunity, while the reported mixture establishes that selectivity is incomplete.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Photodeoxygenation to gamma-butyrothiolactam\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_005\",\n        \"mol_017\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic H2S/HS\u2212 medium, proposed pH 7.5-9, with laboratory-reference irradiation at 254 nm. Establish an irradiation-dose window that favors deoxygenation rather than extensive decomposition. Sulfur precipitation and UV attenuation require monitoring. Actual solar-spectrum performance is unresolved.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photochemical reduction replaces the alpha hydroxyl group with hydrogen while retaining the cyclic thioamide. Formal bookkeeping is 8 C4H7NOS + 8 H2S -> 8 C4H7NS + 8 H2O + S8.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The reported deoxygenated thioamide product is gamma-butyrothiolactam (Patel et al., 2015). This supports selecting the cyclic branch, but does not imply that the thiolactam was the only organic product or that an isolated cyclic precursor was converted quantitatively. The hydroxyl-derived stereocenter is removed here, so it cannot be used to claim an inherited L-proline preference.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Photochemical thiolactam conversion through an inferred cyclic imine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_004\",\n        \"mol_017\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic H2S/HS\u2212 photochemical medium; proposed pH 7.5-9 and 254 nm laboratory-reference irradiation. HCN is present for immediate capture in rxn_008. The imine is a transient mechanistic node, not an isolated product, transported feedstock, or experimentally measured reservoir. No hydrogenation catalyst is introduced that would encourage further reduction to pyrrolidine.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The thiolactam is proposed to undergo photochemical desulfurization to 1-pyrroline. With elemental sulfur as the formal redox endpoint, the net component change is 8 C4H7NS -> 8 C4H7N + S8. H2S is shown on both sides to retain the required sulfide medium while representing cancellation of donor oxidation and substrate-derived sulfide release; this is not a measured sulfur-speciation mechanism.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The thiolactam-to-aminonitrile transformation is supported, but discrete 1-pyrroline is inferred in the supplied reconstruction rather than demonstrated as an isolated intermediate (Patel et al., 2015). This reaction and rxn_008 are therefore mechanistic subdivisions of a precursor-forming operation, not two independently validated preparations. Sulfur speciation, transient-imine lifetime, and competing reduction remain unresolved. Immediate in-place capture addresses the survival problem without assuming successful imine transport.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker-type capture of the cyclic imine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_013\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same local reaction volume as rxn_007, approximately pH 7-9, with HCN/CN\u2212 available during imine generation. No chiral catalyst, chiral feedstock, or imposed circularly polarized irradiation is assumed. The aminonitrile pool is racemic; its R:S ratio is constrained to 1:1 independently of elemental balancing.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide attacks the cyclic imine/iminium carbon, followed by proton transfer, to form pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile. Attack on either enantiotopic face generates the R and S aminonitriles equally in the achiral model.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Formation of the proline aminonitrile is the experimentally supported endpoint of the supplied stepwise route (Patel et al., 2015). The reported 32% over the final two precursor-forming operations must not be assigned separately to either inferred elementary step or to free-proline production. Proline aminonitrile has unusually favorable retention against the cyanohydrin reversion affecting acyclic aminonitriles under low-ammonia conditions (Ritson, 2021). That advantage does not establish hydrolysis yield or enantioenrichment.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-promoted hydration of proline aminonitrile to prolinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_005\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_014\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium supplied by an acidic volcanic-water reservoir\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K; proposed hydrolysis screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ion\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 0.5-2 in a warm, UV-shielded acidic pool receiving the aminonitrile-containing solution by short local flow. Such acidity is a local volcanic assumption, not a general ocean condition. Establish kinetics by time-course measurements rather than assigning an unsupported residence time. Measure residual HCN/H2S loss, competing decomposition, and amide accumulation. Neutral parent formulas stand for the protonated species actually present.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nitrile protonation facilitates water addition, followed by proton transfers and imidic-acid-to-amide tautomerization. Net component change: C5H8N2 + H2O -> C5H10N2O.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a conventional nitrile-hydration extrapolation, not a demonstrated proline-producing step in the supplied cyanosulfidic experiments. Patel et al. (2015) identify the aminonitrile as a precursor without supplying the required hydrolysis yield. General Strecker chemistry supports hydrolytic finishing in principle (Miller, 1957), but does not establish substrate-specific kinetics in this sulfur-rich mixture. The bottleneck is explicitly retained rather than bypassed with an unvalidated sulfur-assisted alternative.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Prolinamide hydrolysis to racemic proline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_005\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium supplied by an acidic volcanic-water reservoir\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K; proposed hydrolysis screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ion\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Continue aqueous hydrolysis in the same UV-shielded acidic setting, approximately pH 0.5-2. Ammonia is predominantly ammonium under these conditions, so acid neutralization and buffer capacity must be included experimentally. Determine conversion, degradation, and racemization rather than assuming quantitative completion. Require a 1:1 L:D product ratio for the achiral model. Analyze free and hydrolyzable proline separately using methods capable of detecting secondary amino groups.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water attacks the protonated amide carbonyl; collapse of the tetrahedral intermediate releases the amino nitrogen as ammonia/ammonium and forms the carboxylic acid. Racemate bookkeeping is 2 C5H10N2O + 2 H2O -> L-C5H9NO2 + D-C5H9NO2 + 2 NH3. Equal enantiomer production is a stereochemical constraint, not a result inferred from atom balance.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Amide hydrolysis is chemically conventional, but its yield and compatibility with this precursor mixture are not established by Patel et al. (2015). Thus the network reaches free proline conditionally, not as an experimentally completed geological synthesis. L-proline is obtained only as a racemate component. Klussmann et al. (2006) demonstrate proline phase-behavior amplification in specified solvent systems, not an established aqueous prebiotic route from a racemate to isolated L-proline. Secondary-amine-compatible analysis is essential because OPA/NAC methods do not detect proline (Parker et al., 2011).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfidic precursor synthesis with unquantified hydrolytic finishing\",\n      \"description\": \"The single intermediate-resolved candidate supported by the supplied research. Acetylene and HCN provide the carbon skeleton; ammonia supplies the ring nitrogen; sulfide-driven photochemistry produces the cyclic thioamide and then the proline aminonitrile. Conventional hydrolysis through prolinamide conditionally gives equal amounts of L- and D-proline. The cyclic imine is inferred, the acidic finishing conditions are proposed rather than verified, and no enantiomeric resolution is claimed. Spark-discharge detections, extraterrestrial detections, and unresolved glutamate or ornithine transformations are not relabeled as additional demonstrated pathways.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_006\",\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_014\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_001\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_004\",\n    \"mol_005\",\n    \"mol_017\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_007\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_006\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "L-Proline", "overall_strategy": "The supplied evidence supports one intermediate-resolved candidate synthesis, not ten independently supported pathways. This network therefore contains one traced pathway rather than inventing nine additional routes or counting changes of catalyst, temperature, or workup as independent syntheses. It does not meet the requested ten-pathway count. The productive sequence is acetylene hydrocyanation, ammonia addition, sulfide-driven photoreductive homologation, cyclic thioamide formation, photochemical conversion to the proline aminonitrile, and hydrolysis through prolinamide. Experimental support is strongest through the aminonitrile; its hydrolysis in the actual reaction mixture remains unquantified. L-proline is represented explicitly as one component of racemic proline, alongside D-proline: this is not an enantioselective synthesis or an isolation of pure L-proline. An aqueous, geochemically established resolution step is unavailable from the supplied evidence, so none is invented. Numerical conditions below are proposed screening windows, not verified reproductions of the Patel supplementary procedures. All operations occupy adjacent surface environments; no transport of unstable intermediates through hydrothermal systems is assumed. Parent neutral formulas are used for component bookkeeping, while pH-dependent protonation is described in conditions. S8 is a formal elemental-sulfur endpoint, not a claim that sulfur speciation was experimentally established. The graph follows productive channels and necessary coproducts, not every component of the experimental mixtures. Because the prescribed role vocabulary lacks 'byproduct', non-target terminal coproducts are labeled 'intermediate'.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "C2H2", "iupac_name": "ethyne", "common_name": "acetylene", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2/c1-2/h1-2H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "sulfane", "common_name": "hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C3H3N", "iupac_name": "prop-2-enenitrile", "common_name": "acrylonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H3N/c1-2-3-4/h2H,1H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C3H6N2", "iupac_name": "3-aminopropanenitrile", "common_name": "beta-aminopropionitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C3H7NO", "iupac_name": "3-aminopropanal", "common_name": "beta-aminopropionaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C4H8N2O", "iupac_name": "4-amino-2-hydroxybutanenitrile", "common_name": "3-aminopropanal cyanohydrin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C4H7NOS", "iupac_name": "3-hydroxypyrrolidine-2-thione", "common_name": "cyclic alpha-hydroxythioamide", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C4H7NS", "iupac_name": "pyrrolidine-2-thione", "common_name": "gamma-butyrothiolactam", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C4H7N", "iupac_name": "3,4-dihydro-2H-pyrrole", "common_name": "1-pyrrol