 survives subsequent sulfide exposure."}, "mechanism": "Copper-mediated addition of HCN across the acetylene triple bond produces acrylonitrile. The net component equation is HCN + C2H2 -> C3H3N.", "reasoning": "The acetylene-to-acrylonitrile entry is described in the cyanosulfidic framework (Patel et al., 2015). Copper is justified specifically for this transformation, not added as a generic mineral catalyst. Its availability and speciation remain geochemical constraints: sulfide can remove dissolved copper, and concentrated cyanide can alter or precipitate copper complexes. The numerical window is a proposed test range because the supplied review did not verify the original experimental procedure."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Ammonia aza-Michael addition to acrylonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K; proposed screening window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic water, approximately pH 9-10, with ammonia in excess over acrylonitrile. Ammonia-rich conditions are staged after copper-mediated coupling rather than assumed compatible with every upstream condition. Quantify secondary Michael addition, oligomerization, and ammonia loss. Do not introduce cyanamide, which would create a competing guanidinylation branch."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia attacks the beta carbon of acrylonitrile, followed by proton transfer to give 3-aminopropanenitrile.", "reasoning": "This addition supplies the nitrogen that ultimately becomes the pyrrolidine ring nitrogen and is part of the reported proline-precursor sequence (Patel et al., 2015). No mineral catalyst is required. Ammonia excess is chemically motivated by competition with further addition of the newly formed primary amine, but the required concentration in a natural pool is not established."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Sulfide-driven photoreduction of the amino nitrile to an amino aldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_004", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_003", "mol_017"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K; proposed screening window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, shallow irradiated water; proposed pH 7.5-9. H2S/HS\u2212 supplies reducing equivalents. Laboratory precedent uses 254 nm irradiation; a productive rate under an early-Earth solar spectrum must be tested separately. Do not require residual dissolved copper. Deliver HCN for immediate downstream aldehyde trapping; avoid isolation, storage, or hot transport of the amino aldehyde. Control optical attenuation by sulfur-bearing solids."}, "mechanism": "Photogenerated reducing equivalents convert the nitrile into an imine-level intermediate, whose hydrolysis releases the aldehyde and ammonia. Formal sulfur-endpoint bookkeeping is 8 C3H6N2 + 8 H2S + 8 H2O -> 8 C3H7NO + 8 NH3 + S8.", "reasoning": "Reductive homologation through aldehydes is supported within the cyanosulfidic framework (Patel et al., 2015). H2S-driven photochemistry need not assume an active Cu(I)/Cu(II) catalyst after sulfide addition, although substrate-specific performance without copper requires confirmation. Sulfidic-anion availability is a genuine environmental constraint rather than an unlimited reagent supply (Ranjan et al., 2018). Aldehyde accumulation, overreduction, and irradiation-dose dependence remain experimental questions."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Cyanohydrin trapping and one-carbon homologation", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K; proposed screening window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Same local reaction volume as aldehyde formation, approximately pH 7-9. HCN/CN\u2212 must be available during aldehyde production. Monitor competing Strecker chemistry, aldehyde oligomerization, and cyanohydrin reversal. The hydroxyl-bearing stereocenter is unspecified and is subsequently erased by deoxygenation."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds to the aldehyde carbonyl and the alkoxide is protonated, forming 4-amino-2-hydroxybutanenitrile.", "reasoning": "Cyanohydrin formation extends the C3 precursor to the C4 skeleton needed for the ring and is part of the supplied reconstruction of Patel et al. (2015). Immediate capture is required because the amino aldehyde is not a credible transported or stockpiled intermediate. Cyanide concentration and ammonia competition must be evaluated in the actual mixture; a connected graph alone does not establish productive flux."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Sulfide addition and formation of the cyclic alpha-hydroxythioamide", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K; proposed screening window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic sulfide-bearing water; proposed pH screening 8-9.5. This node represents the productive cyclic-product channel, not quantitative conversion of the whole mixture. Open-chain hydroxythioamide formation competes. Excess ammonia from earlier stages may influence ring closure and must be measured rather than ignored."}, "mechanism": "Sulfide addition to the nitrile enables thioimidate/thioamide chemistry and intramolecular capture by the terminal amino group. The net cyclic-product channel eliminates ammonia: C4H8N2O + H2S -> C4H7NOS + NH3. The exact order of the elementary sulfur-addition and ring-closure events is not asserted.", "reasoning": "The supplied account reports cyclic and open-chain alpha-hydroxythioamides together (Patel et al., 2015). Consequently, this network does not invent an obligatory, independently demonstrated conversion of an isolated open-chain thioamide into the ring. Five-membered-ring formation explains the chemical opportunity, while the reported mixture establishes that selectivity is incomplete."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Photodeoxygenation to gamma-butyrothiolactam", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_005", "mol_017"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K; proposed screening window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic H2S/HS\u2212 medium, proposed pH 7.5-9, with laboratory-reference irradiation at 254 nm. Establish an irradiation-dose window that favors deoxygenation rather than extensive decomposition. Sulfur precipitation and UV attenuation require monitoring. Actual solar-spectrum performance is unresolved."}, "mechanism": "Photochemical reduction replaces the alpha hydroxyl group with hydrogen while retaining the cyclic thioamide. Formal bookkeeping is 8 C4H7NOS + 8 H2S -> 8 C4H7NS + 8 H2O + S8.", "reasoning": "The reported deoxygenated thioamide product is gamma-butyrothiolactam (Patel et al., 2015). This supports selecting the cyclic branch, but does not imply that the thiolactam was the only organic product or that an isolated cyclic precursor was converted quantitatively. The hydroxyl-derived stereocenter is removed here, so it cannot be used to claim an inherited L-proline preference."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Photochemical thiolactam conversion through an inferred cyclic imine", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_004", "mol_017"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K; proposed screening window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic H2S/HS\u2212 photochemical medium; proposed pH 7.5-9 and 254 nm laboratory-reference irradiation. HCN is present for immediate capture in rxn_008. The imine is a transient mechanistic node, not an isolated product, transported feedstock, or experimentally measured reservoir. No hydrogenation catalyst is introduced that would encourage further reduction to pyrrolidine."}, "mechanism": "The thiolactam is proposed to undergo photochemical desulfurization to 1-pyrroline. With elemental sulfur as the formal redox endpoint, the net component change is 8 C4H7NS -> 8 C4H7N + S8. H2S is shown on both sides to retain the required sulfide medium while representing cancellation of donor oxidation and substrate-derived sulfide release; this is not a measured sulfur-speciation mechanism.", "reasoning": "The thiolactam-to-aminonitrile transformation is supported, but discrete 1-pyrroline is inferred in the supplied reconstruction rather than demonstrated as an isolated intermediate (Patel et al., 2015). This reaction and rxn_008 are therefore mechanistic subdivisions of a precursor-forming operation, not two independently validated preparations. Sulfur speciation, transient-imine lifetime, and competing reduction remain unresolved. Immediate in-place capture addresses the survival problem without assuming successful imine transport."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Strecker-type capture of the cyclic imine", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K; proposed screening window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Same local reaction volume as rxn_007, approximately pH 7-9, with HCN/CN\u2212 available during imine generation. No chiral catalyst, chiral feedstock, or imposed circularly polarized irradiation is assumed. The aminonitrile pool is racemic; its R:S ratio is constrained to 1:1 independently of elemental balancing."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide attacks the cyclic imine/iminium carbon, followed by proton transfer, to form pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile. Attack on either enantiotopic face generates the R and S aminonitriles equally in the achiral model.", "reasoning": "Fo