red HCN production and gas-to-water capture efficiency, supported by source-specific experiments.\",\n        \"Test whether the collected discharge mixture supports copper-mediated hydrocyanation and subsequent photochemistry without an unstated purification step.\",\n        \"Quantify the common hydrolysis endpoint and distinguish free proline from material released only by analytical hydrolysis.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.9,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.79,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.88,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.78,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.81,\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.67\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.8,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.87,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.62,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.8,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.77,\n      \"feedback\": \"rxn_012 conserves atoms and describes a chemically possible energy-driven HCN source. Its usefulness depends on nitrogen activation, discharge product distributions and recovery of sufficient HCN, none of which is specified. The generic Surface label does not establish these operating conditions. Once suitable HCN is available, the downstream skeleton construction is the same supported route as P1, including its unresolved final hydrolysis.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Document CH4:N2 composition, energy input, quenching and measured recoverable HCN rather than relying on the balanced source equation.\",\n        \"Demonstrate collection into a solution with suitable cyanide concentration and speciation for rxn_001.\",\n        \"Establish reaction-specific surface conditions and quantitative aminonitrile hydrolysis.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.9,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.79,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.88,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.78,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.81,\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.68\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.81,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.87,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.63,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.8,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.78,\n      \"feedback\": \"Methane discharge can plausibly supply acetylene, but rxn_013 is an energy-dependent net production channel, not an ordinary ambient aqueous reaction. Acetylene selectivity, cooling and delivery to the copper-mediated coupling are unspecified. These are implementation uncertainties rather than conservation failures. The downstream route remains plausible but does not establish an end-to-end proline yield.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Measure acetylene production and survival through discharge cooling, including competing hydrocarbons and carbonaceous products.\",\n        \"Specify acetylene gas transfer, dissolution and retention during hydrocyanation.\",\n        \"Validate final hydrolysis in material produced by the complete sequence.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.9,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.79,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.88,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.78,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.81,\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.95\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.85,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.89,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.69,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.8,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.81,\n      \"feedback\": \"Calcium carbide hydrolysis is chemically well established and provides a credible acetylene source given the declared starting material. Its alkaline, exothermic source mixture nevertheless requires an explicit connection to downstream conditions. Gas transfer and direct slurry mixing have different consequences for cyanide and copper speciation. The added source step does not resolve the shared uncertainty in proline-producing hydrolysis.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_014\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify whether acetylene is transferred as gas or delivered with carbide-hydrolysis liquid, and establish the resulting pH and material balance.\",\n        \"Document accessible carbide quantity and acetylene recovery for the proposed setting without assuming quantitative delivery.\",\n        \"Test the common nitrile and amide hydrolysis steps under independently justified acidic conditions.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.9,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.79,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.88,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.78,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.81,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.72,\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.68\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.8,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.85,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.6,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.8,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.76,\n      \"feedback\": \"Both discharge source channels are chemically plausible net reactions. Their encoded stoichiometries do not establish simultaneous production, independently adjustable yields or suitable delivery ratios. If they share a discharge environment, their product distributions may be coupled; if separate, collection and mixing must be specified. The uncertainty concerns demonstrated supply and compatibility, not the number of processing steps.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Determine whether the HCN and acetylene sources operate together or separately and measure the corresponding recoverable product distributions.\",\n        \"Establish feed delivery sufficient for hydrocyanation and both later HCN additions without assuming independently controllable source channels.\",\n        \"Carry representative source mixtures through the backbone and quantify proline formation after hydrolysis.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.9,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.79,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.88,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.78,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.81,\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.67,\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.68\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.78,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.84,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.58,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.8,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.75,\n      \"feedback\": \"This implementation requires useful recoveries from both nitrogen-activating HCN production and methane-to-acetylene discharge chemistry. Both channels are balanceable and possible, but their joint performance and collection are unsupported by the supplied reaction records. Listing them as separate net reactions does not establish compatible source fluxes. The common hydrolysis endpoint remains an additional unresolved requirement.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Measure HCN and acetylene yields across the actual gas composition and discharge conditions, including quench and collection losses.\",\n        \"Test recovered mixtures directly in rxn_001 and document any necessary separation or concentration process.\",\n        \"Provide a complete material balance through aminonitrile formation and subsequent proline-producing hydrolysis.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.9,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.79,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.88,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.78,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.81,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.72,\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.95\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.82,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.86,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.62,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.8,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.78,\n      \"feedback\": \"Carbide hydrolysis supplies acetylene through strong established chemistry, while methane-ammonia discharge provides a plausible but unquantified HCN source. Their combination is not intrinsically incompatible. However, collection, source-mixture contamination and the alkaline carbide effluent must be reconciled with hydrocyanation conditions. Neither supply option supplies new evidence for the shared final hydrolysis.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_014\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Quantify recovered HCN from rxn_011 and acetylene delivery from rxn_014 under the stated environmental implementation.\",\n        \"Specify source-stream mixing or gas-transfer arrangements and verify the resulting copper and cyanide speciation.\",\n        \"Measure proline formation under a justified acidification and residence-time regime.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.9,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.79,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.88,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.78,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.81,\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.67,\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.95\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.8,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.85,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.59,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.8,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.76,\n      \"feedback\": \"The acetylene-generating reaction is robust given calcium carbide, but the nitrogen-discharge HCN source lacks operating and recovery evidence. The connection between captured HCN, alkaline carbide-derived material and active copper hydrocyanation is also unspecified. These uncertainties affect implementation without invalidating the balanced graph or the downstream carbon-skeleton sequence.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_014\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Provide experimentally supported HCN generation and collection data for the CH4/N2 discharge channel.\",\n        \"Demonstrate acetylene transfer and pH compatibility rather than assuming unrestricted mixing of source effluents.\",\n        \"Validate the common hydrolysis endpoint and report recoverable L-proline as a component of the total racemic product.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"